  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4,$ we plug $a=4$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{\u2032}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(4),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(4),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} & \u27f9 &  & f(4) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(4),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(4),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(4)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-(\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-4)^{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
